/*
 * ============================================================================
 * Zamani Universal Programming Language
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * File:
 *     grammar/interoperability/interoperability.g4
 *
 * Grammar:
 *     Interoperability
 *
 * Role:
 *     Canonical interoperability composition/orchestration grammar.
 *
 * Status:
 *     PRODUCTION COMPOSITION BOUNDARY
 *
 * ANTLR:
 *     ANTLR4 parser grammar
 *
 * Rust baseline:
 *     Rust 1.97+
 *     Rust 2021
 *     Safe Rust only
 *     No unsafe Rust required
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FEATURE CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * PURPOSE
 * -------
 *
 * This file is the SINGLE COMPOSITION/ORCHESTRATION BOUNDARY for the
 * Zamani interoperability subsystem.
 *
 * It does not implement the individual interoperability languages,
 * representations, ABIs, FFI systems, or external formats.
 *
 * Instead it composes their independently owned parser grammars into stable
 * parser-facing entry points consumed by the canonical Zamani parser.
 *
 *
 * ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLE
 * -----------------------
 *
 *     leaf grammar
 *          |
 *          v
 *     interoperability composition
 *          |
 *          v
 *     ZamaniParser
 *          |
 *          v
 *     domain-neutral AST
 *          |
 *          v
 *     structural validation
 *          |
 *          v
 *     semantic model
 *          |
 *          +-------------------+-------------------+
 *          |                   |                   |
 *          v                   v                   v
 *      classical          quantum::ir          HDL/hardware
 *          |                   |                   |
 *          +-------------------+-------------------+
 *                              |
 *                              v
 *                    canonical/domain IR
 *                              |
 *                     optimization
 *                              |
 *                     lowering
 *                              |
 *                  routing/scheduling
 *                              |
 *                   resilience/QEC/ZQN
 *                              |
 *                             HAL
 *                              |
 *                       target realization
 *
 *
 * THIS FILE OWNS
 * --------------
 *
 * Only composition:
 *
 *     interoperabilityDeclaration
 *     interoperabilityStatement
 *     interoperabilityExpression
 *
 * plus stable, uniquely named orchestration aliases which expose complete
 * interoperability families without taking ownership of their internals.
 *
 *
 * THIS FILE DOES NOT OWN
 * ----------------------
 *
 * It does NOT own:
 *
 *     identifiers
 *     qualified names
 *     attributes
 *     expressions
 *     types
 *     functions
 *     parameters
 *     modules
 *     effects
 *     capabilities
 *     resources
 *     contracts
 *     policies
 *     provenance
 *
 * It also does not own:
 *
 *     FFI syntax
 *     ABI syntax
 *     foreign functions
 *     foreign types
 *     foreign identities
 *     calling conventions
 *     data layout
 *     linkage
 *     serialization
 *     C syntax
 *     C++ syntax
 *     Rust syntax
 *     Python syntax
 *     Zig syntax
 *     QIR syntax
 *     QASM interoperability syntax
 *     WASM interoperability syntax
 *     HDL interoperability syntax
 *     API contracts
 *     system-interface syntax
 *
 * Those remain owned by their dedicated grammars.
 *
 *
 * NON-GOALS
 * ---------
 *
 * This grammar does not:
 *
 *     load libraries
 *     resolve symbols
 *     invoke foreign code
 *     invoke system calls
 *     access files
 *     access networks
 *     discover hardware
 *     discover devices
 *     select targets
 *     allocate resources
 *     perform linking
 *     perform loading
 *     perform ABI lowering
 *     perform serialization
 *     perform deserialization
 *     perform quantum routing
 *     perform scheduling
 *     perform QEC
 *     produce ZQN
 *     access HAL
 *     execute runtime operations
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * AUTHORITY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Global architecture:
 *
 *     grammar/DESIGN.md
 *
 * Normative specifications:
 *
 *     grammar/specification/
 *
 * Formal subsystem contracts:
 *
 *     grammar/spec/
 *
 * Canonical root composition:
 *
 *     grammar/Zamani.g4
 *
 * Canonical ANTLR parser:
 *
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniParser.g4
 *
 * Canonical lexical vocabulary:
 *
 *     grammar/antlr/ZamaniLexer.g4
 *
 * Rust frontend:
 *
 *     src/lexer.rs
 *     src/parser.rs
 *     src/ast/
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * LEXER CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * All parser grammars in this composition use:
 *
 *     ZamaniLexer
 *
 * No interoperability-specific lexer is introduced here.
 *
 * External-language-specific lexical vocabularies are handled by their
 * dedicated external-format frontends where necessary.
 *
 * This file therefore contains NO lexer rules.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * IMPORT CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Only parser grammars may participate in this ANTLR parser composition.
 *
 * Parser-grammar leaves integrated here include:
 *
 *     Ffi
 *     Abi
 *     InteroperabilityForeign
 *     ForeignFunctions
 *     InteroperabilityForeignTypes
 *     InteroperabilityCallingConventions
 *     InteroperabilityDataLayout
 *     InteroperabilityLinkage
 *     SerializationInterop
 *     Api
 *     CInterop
 *     CppInterop
 *     PythonInterop
 *     Rust
 *     Zig
 *     Qir
 *     Qasm
 *     Wasm
 *     HdlInterop
 *     SystemInterfaces
 *
 *
 * IMPORTANT
 * ---------
 *
 * Some repository files are complete external-format grammars rather than
 * parser-grammar leaves intended for import into this composition grammar.
 *
 * These include, where applicable:
 *
 *     openqasm.g4
 *     verilog.g4
 *     AssemblyLanguage.g4
 *     SystemInterfaces.g4
 *
 * Such grammars remain independent format frontends.
 *
 * Their source/format representations must converge through the semantic
 * interoperability model rather than being illegally imported into this
 * parser grammar.
 *
 * The parser-facing adapters are:
 *
 *     qasm.g4
 *     hdl.g4
 *     qir.g4
 *     system-interfaces.g4
 *
 * This distinction prevents combined-grammar imports, duplicate grammar
 * identities, and second parser roots.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * ANTLR COMPOSITION
 * ============================================================================
 */

parser grammar Interoperability;

options {
    tokenVocab = ZamaniLexer;
}

import
    Ffi,
    Abi,
    InteroperabilityForeign,
    ForeignFunctions,
    InteroperabilityForeignTypes,
    InteroperabilityCallingConventions,
    InteroperabilityDataLayout,
    InteroperabilityLinkage,
    SerializationInterop,
    Api,
    CInterop,
    CppInterop,
    PythonInterop,
    Rust,
    Zig,
    Qir,
    Qasm,
    Wasm,
    HdlInterop,
    SystemInterfaces
;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * CANONICAL PARSER BOUNDARIES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * These three rules are the ONLY stable general interoperability entry points
 * that the canonical Zamani parser needs.
 *
 * ZamaniParser.g4 consumes these rules.
 *
 * They deliberately remain separate because declarations, statements, and
 * expressions have different structural and AST ownership.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 */


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * DECLARATIONS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Complete declaration forms are delegated to their owners.
 *
 * This rule must never duplicate a leaf grammar production.
 */
interoperabilityDeclaration
    : abiDeclaration
    | ffiForeignDeclaration
    | foreignFunctionDeclaration
    | interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    | serializationInteroperabilityContract

    | apiDeclaration

    | cInteropDeclaration
    | cppInterop
    | pythonInterop
    | rustInterop

    /*
     * Zig's complete `zigSource` contains EOF and therefore cannot be embedded
     * directly into a Zamani compilation unit.
     *
     * `zigDeclaration` is the reusable declaration boundary.
     */
    | zigDeclaration

    | qirInteropDeclaration
    | qasmDeclaration
    | wasmInteropDeclaration
    | hdlInteroperability

    | systemInterfaceDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * STATEMENTS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Statement-level operations remain owned by their respective leaf grammars.
 */
interoperabilityStatement
    : ffiCallStatement
    | ffiCallbackCallStatement
    | serializationInteroperabilityStatement
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * EXPRESSIONS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Expression-level operations remain owned by their respective leaf grammars.
 */
interoperabilityExpression
    : ffiCallExpression
    | ffiCallbackCallExpression
    | ffiCallableReferenceExpression
    | serializationInteroperabilityExpression
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * STABLE FAMILY ENTRY POINTS
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * These aliases are intentionally uniquely named.
 *
 * They do NOT redefine the syntax of their owning grammars.
 *
 * They exist so tooling, validation, diagnostics, tests, and future grammar
 * composition can refer to a complete interoperability family through one
 * stable orchestration boundary.
 *
 * ============================================================================
 */


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FFI
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityFfiItem
    : ffiItem
    ;

interoperabilityFfiDeclaration
    : ffiForeignDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityFfiStatement
    : ffiCallStatement
    | ffiCallbackCallStatement
    ;

interoperabilityFfiExpression
    : ffiCallExpression
    | ffiCallbackCallExpression
    | ffiCallableReferenceExpression
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ABI
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityAbiDeclaration
    : abiDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityAbiContract
    : abiContract
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FOREIGN IDENTITY
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityForeignIdentity
    : foreignIdentity
    ;

interoperabilityForeignSource
    : foreignSource
    ;

interoperabilityForeignSourceReference
    : foreignSourceReference
    ;

interoperabilityForeignReference
    : foreignReference
    ;

interoperabilityForeignTargetReference
    : foreignTargetReference
    ;

interoperabilityForeignImplementationReference
    : foreignImplementationReference
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FOREIGN FUNCTIONS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * IMPORTANT:
 *
 * Do not create a second foreign-function grammar here.
 */
interoperabilityForeignFunction
    : foreignFunctionDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * FOREIGN TYPES
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * IMPORTANT:
 *
 * Do not recreate `interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration` here.
 *
 * The previous implementation contained an invalid recursive alias:
 *
 *     interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
 *         : interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
 *         ;
 *
 * That is deliberately removed.
 */
interoperabilityForeignType
    : interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityForeignTypeMember
    : interoperabilityForeignTypeMemberDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityForeignTypeReference
    : interoperabilityForeignTypeReference
    ;


/*
 * NOTE:
 *
 * The rule above would itself be recursive if the imported grammar exported
 * a rule with the same name. Therefore this orchestration grammar intentionally
 * does NOT expose a generic alias with that exact imported name.
 *
 * The canonical foreign-type declaration entry remains:
 *
 *     interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
 *
 * supplied directly by InteroperabilityForeignTypes.
 *
 * The public stable family entry is therefore:
 */

interoperabilityForeignTypeItem
    : interoperabilityForeignTypeDeclaration
    | interoperabilityForeignTypeMemberDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * CALLING CONVENTIONS
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityCallingConvention
    : interoperabilityCallingConventionDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityCallingConventionClause
    : interoperabilityCallingConventionClause
    ;


/*
 * IMPORTANT:
 *
 * The imported calling-conventions grammar already owns:
 *
 *     interoperabilityCallingConventionClause
 *
 * A composition grammar must not create an alias having the same name.
 *
 * Consumers should use:
 *
 *     interoperabilityCallingConventionDeclaration
 *
 * or the owning grammar's reusable clause directly.
 *
 *
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * DATA LAYOUT
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityDataLayout
    : dataLayoutDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityDataLayoutContract
    : dataLayoutContract
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * LINKAGE
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityLinkage
    : interoperabilityLinkageDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SERIALIZATION
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * Serialization remains owned by SerializationInterop, which itself provides
 * the interoperability façade over the canonical data serialization grammar.
 *
 * No serialization rule is duplicated here.
 */

interoperabilitySerializationUnit
    : serializationInteroperabilityUnit
    ;

interoperabilitySerializationContract
    : serializationInteroperabilityContract
    ;

interoperabilitySerializationStatement
    : serializationInteroperabilityStatement
    ;

interoperabilitySerializationExpression
    : serializationInteroperabilityExpression
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * API
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityApiDeclaration
    : apiDeclaration
    ;

interoperabilityApiContract
    : apiContract
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * C
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityCDeclaration
    : cInteropDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * C++
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityCppDeclaration
    : cppInterop
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * PYTHON
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityPythonDeclaration
    : pythonInterop
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * RUST
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityRustDeclaration
    : rustInterop
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * ZIG
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * `zigSource` owns an EOF boundary and therefore is an external standalone
 * source root.
 *
 * For embedding inside Zamani, the reusable declaration boundary is used.
 */

interoperabilityZigDeclaration
    : zigDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * QIR
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityQirItem
    : qirInteropItem
    ;

interoperabilityQirDeclaration
    : qirInteropDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * QASM
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityQasmItem
    : qasmInterop
    ;

interoperabilityQasmDeclaration
    : qasmDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * WASM
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 */

interoperabilityWasmDeclaration
    : wasmInteropDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * HDL
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * hdl.g4 is the interoperability adapter over the canonical HDL grammar.
 */
interoperabilityHdl
    : hdlInteroperability
    ;

interoperabilityHdlSource
    : hdlInteroperabilitySource
    ;


/*
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 * SYSTEM INTERFACES
 * ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
 *
 * The parser-grammar source of truth is the lowercase:
 *
 *     system-interfaces.g4
 *
 * The uppercase SystemInterfaces.g4 file is an independent/legacy combined
 * grammar and MUST NOT be imported into this parser grammar.
 *
 * This prevents two grammar identities from representing the same system
 * interface language.
 */
interoperabilitySystemInterface
    : systemInterfaceDeclaration
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * SYMBOLIC REFERENCES
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * These are composition-level references only.
 *
 * They do not select or allocate physical resources.
 *
 * They remain open-world through canonical qualified names and string values.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityAbiReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityCallingConventionReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityDataLayoutReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityLinkageReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityCapabilityReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityResourceReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityFormatReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityRuntimeReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;

interoperabilityProviderReference
    : qualifiedName
    | STRING
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * CANONICAL METADATA BOUNDARY
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * General metadata remains owned by the canonical attribute system.
 *
 * This grammar does not invent an interoperability metadata language.
 * ============================================================================
 */

interoperabilityMetadata
    : attribute
    ;


/*
 * ============================================================================
 * COMPOSITION INVARIANTS
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The following concepts remain semantically distinct:
 *
 *     foreign identity
 *     ABI
 *     calling convention
 *     data layout
 *     linkage
 *     FFI
 *     foreign type
 *     foreign function
 *     API
 *     serialization
 *     external format
 *     system interface
 *
 * A semantic interoperability object may reference several simultaneously.
 *
 * Example conceptual semantic object:
 *
 *     foreign function
 *         |
 *         +--> language identity
 *         +--> API/interface
 *         +--> foreign type
 *         +--> ABI
 *         +--> calling convention
 *         +--> linkage
 *         +--> data layout
 *         +--> effects
 *         +--> capabilities
 *         +--> resources
 *         +--> contracts
 *         +--> policies
 *         +--> provenance
 *
 * The parser preserves structure.
 *
 * Semantic analysis combines the contracts.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * EFFECT INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability constructs may participate in the canonical effect system.
 *
 * Examples include:
 *
 *     foreign
 *     native
 *     io
 *     network
 *     mutation
 *     distributed
 *     measurement
 *     hardware
 *     simulation
 *
 * This grammar does not define effect semantics.
 *
 * It consumes effect-bearing constructs owned by the relevant grammar and
 * leaves effect checking to semantic analysis.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * CAPABILITY INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may require capabilities such as:
 *
 *     foreign.call
 *     native.interop
 *     serialization
 *     system.interface
 *     quantum.interop
 *     hardware.interop
 *     accelerator.interop
 *     distributed.interop
 *
 * Capability names remain open-world symbolic identities.
 *
 * This grammar never grants a capability merely because its name appears in
 * source code.
 *
 * Authorization remains downstream.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * RESOURCE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability may participate in resource requirements such as:
 *
 *     memory
 *     storage
 *     bandwidth
 *     latency
 *     throughput
 *     compute
 *     accelerator availability
 *     communication
 *
 * Resource meaning belongs to the canonical resource subsystem.
 *
 * This grammar introduces no resource ceilings.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * POLICY / SECURITY INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Foreign operations may be constrained by:
 *
 *     security policy
 *     sandbox policy
 *     trust policy
 *     authorization policy
 *     capability policy
 *     resource policy
 *     deployment policy
 *
 * This grammar does not grant permissions.
 *
 * Syntactic validity never implies authorization.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * PROVENANCE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability declarations may contribute provenance information such as:
 *
 *     external identity
 *     provider
 *     source
 *     version
 *     transformation
 *     conversion
 *     generated artifact
 *
 * Provenance is retained by the semantic/frontend pipeline.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * CONTRACT INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability contracts participate in the canonical:
 *
 *     requires
 *     ensures
 *     invariant
 *     assume
 *     guarantee
 *     property
 *     evidence
 *
 * architecture.
 *
 * This grammar does not create a second contract language.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * QUANTUM INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Quantum interoperability is intentionally separated from quantum semantics.
 *
 * External quantum representations may include:
 *
 *     OpenQASM
 *     QIR
 *     vendor formats
 *     simulator formats
 *     provider formats
 *     future quantum formats
 *
 * Their source/format identity remains an interoperability concern.
 *
 * Their computational meaning must converge through:
 *
 *     semantic quantum model
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum::ir
 *
 * This file MUST NOT create:
 *
 *     QuantumInteropIR
 *     QasmIR
 *     QirIR
 *     VendorQuantumIR
 *
 * or any competing quantum semantic representation.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * HDL / HARDWARE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * HDL interoperability may connect:
 *
 *     classical computation
 *     accelerator computation
 *     hardware design
 *     simulation
 *     verification
 *     synthesis
 *     quantum/hybrid control
 *
 * However this composition grammar does not encode:
 *
 *     fixed bus widths
 *     fixed register widths
 *     fixed pin counts
 *     fixed FPGA capacities
 *     fixed ASIC capacities
 *     physical addresses
 *     physical device IDs
 *     physical topology
 *
 * Those belong downstream target/hardware semantics.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * FOREIGN LANGUAGE INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * C, C++, Rust, Python and Zig remain external language boundaries.
 *
 * This file does not attempt to parse their complete languages.
 *
 * Their dedicated interoperability grammars describe only the source-level
 * boundary needed by Zamani.
 *
 * Complete external-language source may instead be parsed by the appropriate
 * external frontend and converted into the canonical Zamani semantic model.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * EXTERNAL FORMAT INTEGRATION
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * OpenQASM, Verilog, assembly, and similar representations may have their own
 * complete external-format grammar.
 *
 * They must not become permanent extensions of the Zamani universal grammar.
 *
 * The integration model is:
 *
 *     external source
 *          |
 *          v
 *     external-format frontend
 *          |
 *          v
 *     format representation
 *          |
 *          v
 *     semantic interoperability validation
 *          |
 *          v
 *     canonical Zamani semantic model
 *
 * For quantum formats:
 *
 *     semantic quantum model
 *          |
 *          v
 *     quantum::ir
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * POCO-REAF CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Interoperability expresses portable intent rather than physical realization.
 *
 * Therefore this file introduces no language-level maximum for:
 *
 *     foreign languages
 *     foreign functions
 *     foreign types
 *     APIs
 *     interfaces
 *     callbacks
 *     parameters
 *     resources
 *     capabilities
 *     serialization contracts
 *     schemas
 *     formats
 *     operations
 *     modules
 *     nodes
 *     devices
 *     processors
 *     qubits
 *     memory
 *     tensor rank
 *     register width
 *
 * There are deliberately no:
 *
 *     MAX_QUBITS
 *     MAX_CPUS
 *     MAX_GPUS
 *     MAX_FPGAS
 *     MAX_NODES
 *     MAX_MEMORY
 *     MAX_THREADS
 *     MAX_TENSOR_RANK
 *     MAX_REGISTER_WIDTH
 *     MAX_NETWORK_SIZE
 *     MAX_DEVICE_COUNT
 *     MAX_FOREIGN_FUNCTIONS
 *     MAX_FOREIGN_TYPES
 *     MAX_INTERFACES
 *     MAX_CALLBACKS
 *
 * Repetition is represented by ANTLR repetition operators in the owning
 * grammars.
 *
 * Physical feasibility is evaluated later from:
 *
 *     capabilities
 *     resources
 *     policies
 *     ABI constraints
 *     runtime constraints
 *     target constraints
 *     deployment constraints
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * DETERMINISM CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Parsing depends only on:
 *
 *     source text
 *     canonical lexer configuration
 *     grammar version
 *     grammar composition
 *
 * Parsing MUST NOT depend on:
 *
 *     hardware availability
 *     filesystem state
 *     network state
 *     environment variables
 *     current time
 *     randomness
 *     linker state
 *     library availability
 *     target availability
 *     runtime state
 *
 * Identical source under identical grammar configuration must produce
 * equivalent parse structure.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * SECURITY / INERTNESS
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This grammar has no runtime side effects.
 *
 * It performs no:
 *
 *     file access
 *     network access
 *     process execution
 *     library loading
 *     symbol resolution
 *     dynamic linking
 *     hardware discovery
 *     device discovery
 *     resource allocation
 *     target selection
 *     foreign invocation
 *     QPU access
 *     linker invocation
 *     loader invocation
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * AST CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * The output of this grammar is parser structure only.
 *
 * AST construction belongs to the existing domain-neutral frontend AST.
 *
 * This file MUST NOT introduce:
 *
 *     C-specific AST
 *     C++-specific AST
 *     Rust-specific AST
 *     Python-specific AST
 *     Zig-specific AST
 *     QIR AST
 *     QASM semantic IR
 *     hardware AST
 *
 * merely because an interoperability grammar is selected.
 *
 * Format-specific intermediate representations may exist in dedicated
 * external frontends, but they must converge into the canonical semantic
 * model before backend lowering.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * SEMANTIC CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * After parsing:
 *
 *     interoperability syntax
 *          |
 *          v
 *     structural validation
 *          |
 *          +--> type analysis
 *          +--> effect analysis
 *          +--> capability analysis
 *          +--> resource analysis
 *          +--> contract analysis
 *          +--> policy/security analysis
 *          +--> provenance
 *          +--> compatibility
 *          |
 *          v
 *     canonical semantic model
 *
 * Interoperability therefore describes a boundary.
 *
 * It does not itself establish that the boundary is executable.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * IR CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This grammar defines no IR.
 *
 * Downstream representation is selected according to semantic domain:
 *
 *     classical
 *         -> canonical classical IR
 *
 *     quantum
 *         -> quantum::ir
 *
 *     HDL/hardware
 *         -> canonical HDL/hardware semantic representation
 *
 *     distributed/networked
 *         -> corresponding canonical semantic model
 *
 * External representations remain adapters.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * RUST CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * This file contains no embedded Rust.
 *
 * Rust implementations consuming generated parser structures must target:
 *
 *     Rust 1.97+
 *     Rust 2021
 *
 * and remain safe Rust.
 *
 * The repository should enforce:
 *
 *     #![forbid(unsafe_code)]
 *
 * at the appropriate crate boundary.
 *
 *
 * ============================================================================
 * COMPATIBILITY CONTRACT
 * ============================================================================
 *
 * Compatibility belongs to:
 *
 *     grammar/compatibility/
 *     grammar/spec/compatibility.md
 *
